{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in the morning watch.</b> One needs to know why HaShem executed this great judgment in the morning and not at night, which is the time when the forces of judgment prevail. I have seen that they said, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni 285), that Gavriel pressed this argument before HaShem, and El Elyon answered him: Wait until that hour when their father acted with Me, as it says: ״וַיַּשְׁכֵּם אַבְרָהָם בַּבֹּקֶר״ (Genesis 22:3) — end of quote. The meaning of HaShem's reply is that although it is not the time of retribution, I am reversing the order for the children of Abraham just as Abraham reversed his merciful nature — and how much more so regarding his beloved son — and I will do likewise, reversing the time to whatever I desire. And there is benefit in this for Israel, that the merit of the moment will stand for them; also, HaShem will perform His good deed in person and with His own honor, just as Abraham did and saddled his donkey himself, and this is what the verse means by <b>and HaShem looked forth</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:04.072963646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}